          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5. What would you observe when zinc is added to a solution of iron(II) sulphate? Write the chemical reaction that takes place.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.275,
            0.632,
            0.845,
            0.653
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3.3 HOW DO METALS AND NON-METALS REA CT?"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.274,
            0.661,
            0.901,
            0.758
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In the above activities, you saw the reactions of metals with a number of reagents. Why do metals react in this manner? Let us recall what we learnt about the electronic configuration of elements in Class IX. We learnt that noble gases, which have a completely filled valence shell, show little chemical activity. We, therefore, explain the reactivity of elements as a tendency to attain a completely filled valence shell.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.275,
            0.758,
            0.899,
            0.79
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us have a look at the electronic configuration of noble gases and some metals and non-metals.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.274,
            0.79,
            0.901,
            0.885
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We can see from Table 3.3 that a sodium atom has one electron in its outermost shell. If it loses the electron from its M shell then its L shell now becomes the outermost shell and that has a stable octet. The nucleus of this atom still has 11 protons but the number of electrons has become 10, so there is a net positive charge giving us a sodium cation Na+. On the other hand chlorine has seven electrons in its outermost shell",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
